@* We removed the unnecessary  VS template content *@

@* 
Adding basic content as with the sample page index.cshtml 
provided to us by default in the "Home"" folder:
    Views/Home/Index.cshtml
*@

@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Music";
}

@* 
  For learning purposes, this View starts with the same layout used by the default MVC template:
    > Views/Home/Index.cshtml
  We will gradually customize it throughout this repository.

  Notice that the generated MVC template already uses Bootstrap for styling and responsive layout.
  The CSS classes below (such as "text-center" and "display-4") come from Bootstrap.

  So MVC template includes Bootstrap
*@
<div class="text-center">
    <h1 class="display-4">Welcome to our music performance</h1>

    <p>
        Learn about <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core">building Web apps with ASP.NET Core</a>.
    </p>

    <hr />
    @* Adding more custom contents below: *@
    <p>You can also review these projects (GitHub) repos:</p>
    <div class="list-group">
        <a href="https://github.com/anmarjarjees/dotnet-csharp-intro" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action" target="_blank">
            .NET and C# Introduction
        </a>
        <a href="https://github.com/anmarjarjees/music-gear-tracker" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action" target="_blank">
            ASP.NET Core MVC - Music Gear Tracker
        </a>
    </div>

    <hr />
    @*
    Accessing data passed from the Controller:
    ******************************************
    In MusicController.cs we stored two values in ViewData:
       > ViewData["MusicMessage"]
       > ViewData["GuitarMessage"]

    ViewData behaves like a dictionary:
        > each value is stored using a key
        > we retrieve it here using the same key
*@
    <h2>Dependency Injection Demo</h2>

    <h3>Music Service</h3>

    @* Using BootStrap alert boxes: *@
    <div class="alert alert-success">
        @ViewData["MusicMessage"]
    </div>

    <h3>Guitar Service</h3>

    <div class="alert alert-info">
        @ViewData["GuitarMessage"]
    </div>
</div>